values
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001'::uuid, 'fog-water-redwoods', 'Water from Fog', 'Ecología de bosques costeros', 'natural_science', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'Along some cool coastlines, summer rain is rare even though thick fog arrives almost every night. Coastal redwoods and several neighboring plants have adapted to this unusual combination. Their leaves intercept tiny droplets suspended in the fog. Some of that water enters the leaves directly, while the rest gathers into larger drops that fall to the forest floor.

This process, known as fog drip, benefits more than the trees that collect the water. Moisture reaching the soil can be absorbed by shallow roots, mosses, and small animals. Researchers have measured noticeably wetter soil beneath redwoods than in nearby open areas. The trees therefore act as structures that redistribute atmospheric water through the ecosystem.

Fog does not replace rainfall entirely. A long period without either rain or fog can still stress the forest. Nevertheless, fog drip helps coastal plants survive the driest part of the year and may become increasingly important where seasonal rainfall becomes less predictable.', 156, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002'::uuid, 'nurse-logs', 'A Fallen Tree’s Second Life', 'Regeneración forestal', 'natural_science', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'When a large tree falls in a wet forest, decay begins almost immediately. Fungi and insects enter the wood, breaking its tissues into smaller components. The fallen trunk gradually becomes soft and porous, so it can hold considerable amounts of rainwater.

Seeds that land on this damp surface sometimes grow more successfully than seeds on the crowded forest floor. The elevated trunk gives young plants access to light and keeps them above layers of wet leaves. As the wood decomposes, it also releases nutrients that the seedlings can use. For these reasons, ecologists often call a fallen trunk that supports new growth a nurse log.

The relationship changes over time. Roots eventually extend through the decaying log and enter the soil below. Decades later, the original trunk may disappear completely, leaving a row of mature trees whose raised roots reveal where their temporary support once lay.', 146, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003'::uuid, 'living-light', 'Living Light in the Ocean', 'Bioluminiscencia marina', 'natural_science', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'Sunlight fades rapidly below the ocean’s surface, yet darkness does not mean that the deep sea is without light. Many organisms produce their own through bioluminescence, a chemical reaction in which energy is released as visible light. Unlike a hot electric bulb, this reaction produces very little heat. It can therefore occur inside delicate tissues without damaging them.

Bioluminescence serves different purposes. Some small animals flash when a predator approaches. The sudden light may startle the attacker or attract a larger animal that then attacks the original predator. Other species use rows of light-producing organs along the underside of their bodies. Seen from below, this glow resembles the faint light descending from the surface and makes the animal’s silhouette harder to detect.

Light can also help organisms find food. Certain fish suspend a glowing structure near the mouth, where it resembles a small prey animal. Curious organisms approach the light and become prey themselves. In other cases, a narrow beam allows a fish to inspect its surroundings without illuminating its entire body.

Because the deep ocean is difficult to observe directly, scientists once learned about these displays mainly from animals brought to the surface. Such specimens were often injured and behaved unnaturally. Modern low-light cameras and quiet remotely operated vehicles now record bioluminescence under the pressure and temperature conditions in which it evolved. These tools have revealed that living light is not an occasional curiosity but a widespread form of communication, concealment, and predation.', 244, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004'::uuid, 'volcanic-soil', 'From Ash to Fertile Soil', 'Formación de suelos volcánicos', 'natural_science', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A fresh layer of volcanic ash looks like an unlikely place for plants. It contains almost no organic matter, holds water poorly, and may shift in the wind. Yet landscapes covered by eruptions can eventually develop unusually productive soils. The change begins with physical and chemical weathering. Rain, temperature changes, and weak acids gradually break volcanic glass and minerals into finer particles.

As those minerals weather, they release elements such as potassium, phosphorus, and iron. These nutrients alone do not create fertile soil, however. Pioneer organisms must first stabilize the surface. Lichens grow directly on exposed particles, while grasses and other hardy plants trap windblown dust. When these organisms die, decomposers convert their remains into organic matter.

The mixture of weathered ash and organic material has a distinctive structure. Tiny mineral particles can bind with decomposed material and create spaces that retain both water and nutrients. In humid regions, this process may produce deep, dark soil. Farmers value such land, although cultivating it carelessly can destroy its advantages. Once protective vegetation is removed, the light particles are vulnerable to erosion.

Not every volcanic deposit follows the same path. Climate, ash composition, slope, and the frequency of later eruptions all affect soil formation. A cool, wet deposit may develop differently from ash of the same age in a dry environment. Thus, the celebrated fertility of volcanic regions is not an immediate gift of an eruption but the result of a long interaction among rock, climate, and living organisms.', 247, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005'::uuid, 'seed-banks', 'Seeds Held in Reserve', 'Conservación de diversidad vegetal', 'natural_science', 'B2', 2, 'short', 'A seed bank stores seeds under carefully controlled conditions so that plant varieties remain available even if they disappear from farms or wild habitats. Before storage, workers dry the seeds to slow their metabolism. They then seal them in moisture-resistant containers and place them in cold rooms. For many species, low temperature and low moisture can extend survival for decades.

Storage is not completely passive. Technicians periodically remove small samples and test whether the seeds still germinate. When germination rates fall, the bank grows a new generation of plants and collects fresh seeds. This regeneration step must be managed carefully: if only a few plants reproduce, some of the original genetic diversity may be lost.

Seed banks are valuable, but they cannot preserve every plant. Some tropical species produce seeds that are damaged by drying or freezing. Other plants reproduce without conventional seeds. These cases require living collections, tissue cultures, or frozen cells. Conservationists therefore treat seed banks as one part of a broader strategy rather than a complete replacement for protecting ecosystems.', 173, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006'::uuid, 'magnetic-migration', 'A Map without Landmarks', 'Orientación magnética de aves', 'natural_science', 'B2', 3, 'standard', 'Migrating birds use several sources of information to remain on course. The position of the sun and stars can provide compass directions, while coastlines, rivers, and mountains offer visible landmarks. These cues are not always available. Clouds may hide the sky, and a young bird crossing an unfamiliar region cannot rely on remembered scenery. Experiments suggest that many species also detect properties of Earth’s magnetic field.

A magnetic compass and a magnetic map are not the same. A compass indicates direction, just as a handheld compass points north. A map provides information about location. Earth’s magnetic field varies gradually across the planet: its lines meet the surface at different angles, and its strength changes from place to place. If birds can sense both properties, they may estimate where they are as well as which way to fly.

Researchers test this possibility by temporarily placing birds in controlled magnetic fields. The birds remain physically in one location, but coils surrounding their enclosure reproduce the magnetic conditions of another region. Some birds then attempt to fly in the direction that would be appropriate from the simulated site rather than from their actual one. This response suggests that they interpreted the artificial field as location information.

The sensory mechanism remains debated. Magnetic particles have been found in some animal tissues, but their exact role is uncertain. Another hypothesis proposes that light-sensitive molecules in the eye create chemical reactions influenced by magnetic direction. These explanations are not necessarily exclusive, and different species may combine them. What is clear is that migration is not guided by a single internal instrument; it depends on a flexible system that compares multiple cues.', 275, 8, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007'::uuid, 'coral-night-calendar', 'A Reef’s Night Calendar', 'Reproducción sincronizada de corales', 'natural_science', 'B2', 3, 'long', 'A coral reef may appear fixed and stone-like, but each coral colony is built by thousands of living animals called polyps. On a small number of nights each year, many colonies release bundles of eggs and sperm into the water at nearly the same time. The event can fill the sea with drifting pink or white particles. Because individual colonies cannot move to find mates, this synchronized spawning greatly increases the chance that reproductive cells from different colonies will meet.

Synchronization occurs at several scales. Seasonal changes in water temperature help determine the month. The lunar cycle narrows the event to particular nights, often a set number of days after a full moon. Finally, the transition from daylight to darkness helps coordinate the hour. Different species may respond to slightly different combinations of these signals, so they can spawn on separate nights or at different times during the same evening.

Releasing reproductive cells together offers advantages beyond finding mates. When billions of bundles enter the water at once, predators cannot consume all of them. This effect, called predator satiation, allows a proportion to survive even when fish and other animals gather to feed. Currents then carry fertilized eggs away from the parent reef. After a period drifting as larvae, survivors settle on suitable hard surfaces and begin new colonies.

The timing is precise but not perfectly rigid. Local conditions can shift an event by a night or more. Heavy rain may lower salinity near shore, and unusually warm or cool water can alter development inside the colonies. Separate reefs in the same region therefore do not always spawn simultaneously. Scientists who want to observe an event combine long-term records with current measurements rather than relying on a calendar date alone.

Climate change complicates these predictions. Corals use temperature as a developmental signal, but extreme heat can also cause bleaching, a breakdown of the relationship between polyps and the microscopic algae that supply much of their energy. A colony under severe stress may produce fewer reproductive cells or skip spawning. Recovery from bleaching can take months, so the reproductive effects may continue after water temperatures return to their usual range. If warming changes temperature patterns differently from lunar and daylight cycles, signals that once coincided may become less closely aligned.

Researchers monitor spawning for practical as well as scientific reasons. During an event, they can collect egg-and-sperm bundles, fertilize them under controlled conditions, and raise young corals for restoration projects. Young colonies produced in this way can be placed on damaged reefs, although their survival is never guaranteed. Teams also compare which parent colonies produce larvae that tolerate warmer conditions. The work is demanding because the opportunity may last only a few hours. Understanding the reef’s multiple clocks helps teams prepare, but successful collection still depends on close observation of local colonies and conditions.', 473, 13, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008'::uuid, 'deep-ocean-carbon', 'The Ocean’s Falling Snow', 'Ciclo de carbono oceánico', 'natural_science', 'C1', 3, 'long', 'In the sunlit upper ocean, microscopic algae use carbon dioxide during photosynthesis. This process converts dissolved carbon into organic material and forms the base of much marine food. Most of that material is consumed and recycled near the surface, but a small fraction begins to sink. Viewed in underwater lights, the descending mixture of dead cells, waste, mucus, and mineral fragments resembles a slow snowfall, so researchers call it marine snow.

Individual particles are tiny and would sink very slowly. They often collide and stick together, however, forming larger aggregates that descend more rapidly. Zooplankton also package fine material into dense waste pellets. Both mechanisms shorten the time during which currents and microbes can keep particles near the surface. The speed of descent matters because bacteria consume organic matter as it falls, returning much of its carbon to dissolved form.

The amount that reaches deep water varies widely. Warm temperatures generally accelerate microbial activity, while the chemical composition of a particle affects how easily it can be broken down. Minerals such as calcium carbonate or windblown dust may add weight and may also shield some organic material from bacteria. Large, dense particles can therefore carry carbon farther than light, fragile ones.

Animals complicate the vertical journey. Some species feed near the surface at night and swim hundreds of meters downward during the day to avoid predators. They carry carbon inside their bodies and release part of it through respiration and waste at depth. This active transport differs from passive sinking, yet it contributes to the same broad movement of carbon away from the atmosphere.

Carbon that enters deep water is not necessarily stored forever. Ocean circulation can eventually bring it back toward the surface, where it may again exchange with the air. The interval can range from decades to centuries depending on depth and location. A much smaller quantity reaches seafloor sediments and becomes buried, where isolation may last far longer.

Scientists want to know whether climate change will strengthen or weaken this biological carbon pump. Warming can increase stratification, reducing the supply of nutrients from deep water to algae at the surface. In some regions, lower nutrient availability may reduce production and sinking. At the same time, shifts in plankton species could change particle size, mineral content, and consumption by animals. These interacting effects make a single global prediction difficult.

Researchers combine sediment traps, optical sensors, chemical measurements, and computer models to estimate the flow. Each method captures only part of the process: traps can disturb currents, cameras do not directly measure carbon, and models depend on assumptions. Agreement among independent methods therefore provides stronger evidence than any one instrument. Marine snow may look gentle, but understanding its descent is central to explaining how the ocean regulates atmospheric carbon over long periods.', 463, 14, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009'::uuid, 'libraries-shared-infrastructure', 'More Than Shelves', 'Funciones contemporáneas de bibliotecas públicas', 'social_science', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'Public libraries were once defined mainly by the books they collected, but their role has expanded as information has moved online. Many now lend electronic materials and provide internet access, computers, and assistance with digital forms. For residents who lack reliable devices or connections at home, these services are basic infrastructure rather than optional conveniences.

Libraries also offer a kind of public space that is increasingly uncommon. Visitors are generally allowed to remain without making a purchase. A student can work quietly, a job seeker can prepare an application, and a recently arrived resident can attend a language class. These activities differ, yet they share the same accessible setting.

This broader mission creates challenges. Staff members need skills that range from preserving local records to teaching digital privacy. Buildings designed for silent reading must accommodate conversation, workshops, and group work without abandoning quiet areas. The modern library therefore balances continuity with adaptation: it protects access to recorded knowledge while changing how that access is delivered.', 165, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010'::uuid, 'markets-and-city-streets', 'Markets That Move', 'Mercados temporales y espacio urbano', 'social_science', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'A street market may occupy a plaza for only a few hours, yet its temporary presence can shape the surrounding neighborhood. Vendors arrive early with tables, shade coverings, and portable displays. Customers alter their usual routes, and nearby shops may experience more foot traffic. When the market closes, the physical structures disappear, but social and economic connections formed there may continue.

Temporary markets lower some barriers faced by small producers. Renting a permanent store requires long contracts and substantial capital, whereas a weekly stall can be tested with less risk. Vendors can observe which products attract buyers and adjust their offerings before making larger investments.

The flexibility also creates tension. Residents may object to noise, blocked parking, or waste, while vendors need predictable rules and access to desirable locations. Successful market programs usually depend on negotiation among organizers, local government, businesses, and residents. The market is therefore not simply a collection of stalls; it is a recurring agreement about how public space will be shared.', 165, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011'::uuid, 'standard-time', 'When Noon Became Standard', 'Estandarización social del tiempo', 'social_science', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'For much of history, towns kept local solar time. Noon occurred when the sun reached its highest point, so clocks in places separated by even modest distances could differ by several minutes. Such differences caused little trouble when travel and communication were slow. A person usually needed to coordinate activities only with others nearby.

Railroads changed that situation in the nineteenth century. Trains crossed many local time systems during a single journey, and printed schedules became difficult to interpret. A departure listed as nine o’clock might refer to station time, railway headquarters time, or the time of another city. Telegraph networks made the inconsistency more visible because messages traveled almost instantly between clocks that disagreed.

Railway companies responded by adopting shared regional times. In some countries, private companies standardized their schedules before national governments established legal time zones. The change was not immediately welcomed. Municipal leaders sometimes saw local time as a symbol of independence, and clock owners had to decide whether to adjust public clocks or display two times.

Eventually, standard time became ordinary enough to seem natural. That familiarity can hide the fact that time zones are negotiated systems rather than direct copies of solar time. Their boundaries bend around political borders and economic relationships, and governments occasionally move them for practical reasons. Standard time solved genuine coordination problems, but it did so by replacing many locally observed noons with a smaller number of socially agreed ones worldwide.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012'::uuid, 'community-mapping', 'Maps Made by Residents', 'Cartografía participativa', 'social_science', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'Official maps are designed for particular purposes, so they inevitably leave some information out. A transportation map may show roads but omit an informal footpath used daily by residents. A land-use map may identify buildings without recording where seasonal flooding blocks an entrance. Community mapping projects invite local people to document such knowledge.

Participants may draw on printed maps, collect coordinates with phones, or annotate photographs. The process matters as much as the finished map. When residents compare observations, they often discover that groups experience the same place differently. A well-lit route preferred by one person may be inaccessible to someone using a wheelchair.

Community knowledge is detailed but not automatically complete or neutral. Meetings can exclude people who lack time, confidence, or social status. Facilitators therefore use multiple ways to contribute and record who participated. The goal is not to replace technical surveys but to place lived experience alongside them, making decisions about public space better informed.', 158, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013'::uuid, 'learning-through-apprenticeship', 'Learning Beside an Expert', 'Aprendizaje por participación', 'social_science', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'An apprenticeship is often described as learning by imitation, but observation alone is rarely enough. Newcomers usually begin with limited, low-risk tasks that contribute to real work. In a bakery, an apprentice might weigh ingredients before mixing dough; in a workshop, the learner may organize tools before operating machinery. These tasks introduce the sequence and standards of the activity without making the beginner responsible for its most dangerous parts.

As competence grows, responsibility expands. Experts demonstrate procedures, but they also make tacit judgments that are difficult to express as rules. A potter adjusts pressure after feeling the clay change; a technician notices a vibration that indicates a machine is misaligned. Learners acquire this knowledge through repeated participation, questions, correction, and comparison of outcomes.

The social structure of the workplace affects what can be learned. An expert who performs every difficult step without explanation may remain impressive but teaches little. Likewise, a learner who fears exposing uncertainty may avoid asking necessary questions. Effective apprenticeships create room for early mistakes whose consequences are manageable and provide timely, specific feedback before those mistakes become lasting habits.

Apprenticeship should not be romanticized. Traditional arrangements have sometimes depended on unpaid labor or excluded people from professions through informal networks. Modern programs attempt to preserve situated practice while adding clear standards, fair compensation, and accessible routes for reporting problems. Their central challenge is to formalize protection without reducing learning to a checklist detached from real work.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014'::uuid, 'bilingual-signs', 'Reading a Bilingual Street', 'Señalización y lenguas en espacio público', 'social_science', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'Bilingual signs do more than repeat the same message in two languages. They help people find essential public services, understand safety information, and participate fully in public life. At the same time, choices about which language appears first, which receives larger letters, and where each language is used communicate something about status and belonging.

The two versions are not always direct translations. A transit agency may use a short standardized term in one language and a fuller explanation in another because readers have different familiarity with the system. A place name may retain an older Indigenous form while practical directions appear in a national language. Designers must consider clarity, available space, reading direction, and the expectations attached to each language.

Researchers sometimes study a city’s “linguistic landscape” by photographing and classifying signs. A count can show where languages are visible, but numbers alone may mislead. Ten small temporary notices do not necessarily carry the same authority as one permanent sign above a government office. Interpretation therefore requires attention to location, material, size, authorship, and audience.

Signage policies can increase access, yet they cannot by themselves guarantee equal treatment. A hospital may display multilingual directions while lacking trained staff who can discuss complex care with patients. Conversely, an unofficial handwritten sign may provide highly useful and timely information within a neighborhood. Public language is best understood not as decoration but as one layer of a larger system of communication and power.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015'::uuid, 'counting-a-population', 'Counting a Moving Population', 'Métodos y efectos de los censos', 'social_science', 'B2', 3, 'long', 'A census aims to describe an entire population at a particular moment. Governments use the results to distribute legislative seats, plan schools and transportation, and compare changes across regions. The apparent simplicity of counting everyone hides a basic difficulty: a population is constantly moving. People are born, die, travel, share homes temporarily, or live in places that official records do not recognize.

To make the task possible, census agencies establish rules about where each person should be counted. A student living away from home, a seasonal worker, or a person staying with relatives could plausibly be associated with more than one address. Most systems choose a concept such as usual residence and specify a reference date. These conventions do not eliminate ambiguity, but they make separate responses more comparable.

Methods of collecting information have changed. Enumerators once visited nearly every household with paper forms. Many agencies now invite online responses first, then send reminders or workers to addresses that have not replied. Administrative records may help identify occupied buildings or check basic details. Using several sources can reduce cost, but it also requires careful procedures to prevent the same person from being counted twice.

Undercounting is not evenly distributed. People in remote areas may be difficult to reach, while those who move frequently may miss forms and follow-up visits. Language barriers, distrust of government, or fear that information will be shared with other agencies can also reduce participation. A high national response rate may therefore coexist with serious gaps in particular communities.

Agencies attempt to measure these gaps after the main count. One method surveys a smaller sample independently and compares its records with census responses. Statistical models can then estimate how many people were probably missed or duplicated. Adjustment is controversial because it replaces some direct counts with estimates, yet leaving known patterns of omission untouched also affects representation and funding.

Question wording creates another source of change. Categories used to describe occupation, household relationships, disability, or identity evolve as societies and policy needs change. A new category may make previously invisible experiences measurable, but it can complicate comparison with earlier censuses. Researchers must decide whether an apparent increase reflects social change, a new definition, or greater willingness to answer.

The census is therefore both a measurement and an institution. Its numbers influence important public decisions, and those decisions can affect whether communities trust the next national count. Public discussion before forms are finalized can expose unclear wording and practical barriers for local residents. Transparency about definitions, privacy protections, uncertainty, and correction methods is essential. No census produces a perfectly still portrait of society, but a carefully designed one can provide a shared reference while making its limitations visible.', 450, 13, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000016'::uuid, 'water-by-agreement', 'Water by Agreement', 'Gobernanza comunitaria del riego', 'social_science', 'C1', 3, 'long', 'In dry farming regions, an irrigation canal is both a physical structure and a schedule of obligations. Water must be diverted from a river, carried across uneven ground, and delivered to fields in an order that users recognize as legitimate. A technically efficient canal can still fail if farmers do not maintain it or believe that distribution is unfair.

Many long-lived systems assign water in turns. A household may receive a flow for a fixed number of hours, or its turn may last until a field reaches a specified point of saturation. The rule chosen reflects local conditions. Time is easier to measure, but equal hours do not deliver equal volumes when flow changes. A field-based rule adapts to need but may be harder for others to verify.

Maintenance is commonly organized as collective labor. Before the growing season, users remove sediment, repair walls, and clear plants from the channel. Participation may be proportional to land area, water rights, or household status. Someone unable to work might contribute money or provide food. These arrangements convert an individual need for water into repeated cooperation.

Monitoring matters because upstream users can take water before it reaches downstream fields. Communities respond with canal guards, rotating inspectors, visible gates, or public records of turns. Effective monitoring does not require constant punishment. When rules and actions are observable, most users can anticipate how violations will be detected, and disputes can be addressed before distrust spreads.

Not all traditions are equitable. Rules inherited from earlier periods may favor large landowners, exclude tenants, or assign obligations differently by gender. Calling a system communal does not guarantee that every affected person has a voice. Reforms sometimes create elected committees or written appeals, but formal procedures can also be captured by people with more time, literacy, or political influence.

External projects can unintentionally weaken local coordination. An agency may line a canal with concrete to reduce leakage but ignore the fact that seepage replenished wells or supported plants used by households. It may install gates that are efficient yet too expensive for local repair. Conversely, romanticizing traditional practice can preserve avoidable losses and unequal rules. Useful intervention begins by understanding both hydraulic flows and social relationships.

Climate variability adds pressure to these institutions. A schedule designed around a reliable snowmelt may become difficult to follow when water arrives earlier or in shorter bursts. Communities may need to revise the length of turns, prioritize crops differently, or reserve water for domestic use. Such changes can impose unequal local costs, so temporary experiments and public review may be especially useful. Whether local adaptation succeeds depends not only on available engineering but on whether users consider the process for changing rules legitimate.', 450, 14, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000017'::uuid, 'roman-concrete', 'Concrete That Heals', 'Materiales de construcción romanos', 'history', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'Roman builders made concrete by mixing lime, water, volcanic ash, and pieces of rock. In harbors, they sometimes placed the mixture directly into wooden forms under seawater. The resulting structures could remain strong for centuries, even while modern concrete exposed to salt water often cracks and weakens.

Microscopic studies have revealed small lime-rich fragments throughout some Roman concrete. These fragments were once dismissed as evidence of careless mixing. Researchers now propose that they can contribute to self-repair. When a crack reaches a fragment and water enters, calcium-rich material dissolves and later forms new minerals inside the opening.

This process cannot repair unlimited damage, and Roman recipes varied widely. Nevertheless, the finding changes how historians interpret apparently imperfect material. What looked like a defect may have resulted from a hot-mixing method that gave the concrete useful long-term behavior.', 137, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018'::uuid, 'relay-posts', 'Messages in Relays', 'Redes postales premodernas', 'history', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'Long before railways or electronic communication, large states developed relay systems for official messages. Stations were placed along major routes, each within reach of the next. A courier arriving with a sealed document could exchange a tired horse for a rested one or hand the message to another rider.

The relay increased speed because no single animal or person had to complete the entire journey without rest. It also required substantial organization. Stations needed food, animals, workers, and accurate information about authorized travelers. Rulers issued tokens or documents that allowed officials to use these resources.

Although relay posts are often described as early postal systems, they were not always open to ordinary correspondence. Their main purpose was administrative: reporting conditions, transmitting orders, and moving officials. The network connected distant regions to a political center, but access to that speed was itself a form of power.', 145, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000019'::uuid, 'paper-travels-west', 'A Material That Changed Writing', 'Difusión histórica del papel', 'history', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'Paper was developed in China from plant fibers and discarded textiles. Compared with bamboo strips, it was light; compared with silk, it was relatively inexpensive. Its manufacture required knowledge of how to separate fibers, suspend them in water, lift them on a screen, press the wet sheet, and dry it evenly.

The technique spread gradually through movement and contact rather than in a single journey. Workshops appeared in Central Asia and later in cities governed by Islamic dynasties. Artisans adapted raw materials to local conditions, using linen and hemp rags where the plants common in Chinese production were unavailable. Knowledge traveled with craftspeople, merchants, prisoners, and written descriptions, although historians debate the importance of each route. Water-powered equipment eventually helped pulp fibers in some regions.

Paper did not immediately replace every writing surface. Papyrus remained important in parts of the Mediterranean, while parchment was valued for durability and could be erased and reused. Institutions selected materials according to price, prestige, availability, and purpose. Administrative offices, merchants, scholars, and religious communities could make different choices.

As paper production expanded, it supported more extensive record keeping and bookmaking. Yet the material alone did not create a literate society. Schools, scribes, trade networks, state demand, and later printing technologies shaped who could produce and read texts. The ability to recycle used fibers also affected local material supply. Paper lowered some production costs, but its historical effect depended on the institutions that used it.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000020'::uuid, 'stars-waves-and-islands', 'Finding Islands without Instruments', 'Navegación tradicional del Pacífico', 'history', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'Navigators in the Pacific traveled between islands across vast stretches of open water without magnetic compasses or modern charts. Their knowledge did not depend on a single sign. Instead, they combined observations of stars, ocean swells, winds, birds, clouds, and the color of the sea.

Stars provided directional reference. A navigator learned where particular stars rose and set and could imagine the canoe at the center of a changing star compass. When clouds hid the sky, long ocean swells could preserve information about distant winds. Several swells might cross at once, so recognizing a useful pattern required experience rather than simply following the largest wave.

Signs of land became important near the end of a voyage. Some seabirds feed far offshore but return to islands at night, and particular cloud formations may gather above land. Reflected light can give the underside of a cloud a pale green tint over a lagoon. None of these signs alone guarantees land, but several consistent observations increase confidence.

Knowledge was carried through teaching, memory, and repeated voyages. Training included names and sequences, but it also developed careful attention to subtle movement at sea. A learner might lie quietly in the canoe and feel how its motion changed as waves struck from different directions. Experienced navigators evaluated learners gradually over many journeys, allowing responsibility to grow as observations became more reliable. Navigation was therefore both an intellectual system and a bodily skill refined through practice.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021'::uuid, 'coffeehouses-and-news', 'Rooms Full of News', 'Cafés y circulación de información', 'history', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'Coffeehouses expanded in several European cities during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Customers came for the drink, but many also came for information. Newspapers, pamphlets, shipping notices, and letters circulated through rooms where strangers could discuss trade, politics, science, and literature.

Different establishments developed particular reputations. Merchants gathered near exchanges and docks, while writers or political groups favored other locations. Regular customers created networks that could spread news rapidly. The same speed also carried rumor, and governments sometimes watched coffeehouses or attempted to restrict discussion.

These spaces were not open to everyone equally. Cost, location, social rules, and gender shaped who could participate. It is therefore misleading to describe every coffeehouse as a fully democratic public sphere. Their historical importance lies instead in how they connected print, conversation, business, and sociability in new combinations.', 134, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022'::uuid, 'calendars-and-seasons', 'Keeping a Calendar in Step', 'Historia de calendarios', 'history', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A calendar divides time into named units, but the astronomical cycles used to define those units do not fit neatly together. A lunar month is about twenty-nine and a half days, while a solar year is roughly 365 and a quarter days. Twelve lunar months therefore end before the seasons complete a full cycle.

Societies have addressed this mismatch in different ways. A purely lunar calendar allows months to move through the seasons. A solar calendar keeps dates aligned with the seasons but does not attempt to match each month to the moon. Lunisolar systems use lunar months and periodically insert an additional month so that seasonal festivals and agricultural markers remain in approximately the same part of the year.

Even solar calendars need correction because a year is not an exact whole number of days. Leap-day rules add time according to a repeating pattern. If the rule adds slightly too much or too little, calendar dates drift slowly relative to equinoxes and solstices. Over centuries, a small annual error becomes noticeable.

Calendar reform is never only an astronomical calculation. Changing a rule can alter religious observances, tax periods, contracts, and anniversaries. Printed schedules and official legal records may also need carefully coordinated conversion across an entire political region. People may strongly resist losing locally familiar dates even when the new system tracks the sun more accurately. A successful reform must therefore coordinate institutions as carefully as it coordinates days.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000023'::uuid, 'archaeology-of-discarded-things', 'What People Threw Away', 'Arqueología de residuos domésticos', 'history', 'B2', 3, 'long', 'Archaeologists often learn about past households from objects their owners no longer wanted. Broken pottery, animal bones, ash, worn tools, and construction debris accumulate in pits or layers. Unlike valuable objects deliberately preserved, ordinary refuse can record repeated activities with little concern for how later observers will interpret it.

A deposit is not a direct photograph of daily life. People sort, reuse, burn, move, and trade discarded material. A cracked vessel may be repaired before it is finally thrown away, and bones may be carried away by animals after disposal. Floods or later construction can mix deposits from different periods. Archaeologists must reconstruct the processes that formed a layer before treating its contents as evidence.

Location provides one clue. Refuse beside a cooking area may differ from material dumped at the edge of a settlement. Tiny fish bones or plant seeds are easily missed during excavation, so soil is often passed through screens or floated in water. Light botanical remains rise to the surface, while heavier fragments sink. Recording the mesh size and sampled volume allows later researchers to evaluate what could have been recovered. These recovery methods can transform an apparently empty sample into evidence about diet and environment.

Quantities also require interpretation. A large number of cattle bones does not automatically mean that every resident ate beef frequently. The bones could come from specialized butchery, a feast, or a workshop processing hides. Researchers compare body parts, cut marks, burning, age of animals, and distribution across sites. Multiple patterns are stronger than a single count.

Refuse can reveal inequality. Fine imported ceramics may cluster around certain houses, while repairs on ordinary vessels can indicate efforts to extend their use. Yet expensive objects do not map perfectly onto wealthy people. An heirloom can remain in a modest household, and a rich household may dispose of goods somewhere not excavated. Interpretation works with probabilities rather than simple labels.

Modern assumptions create another risk. Categories such as useful and waste change across cultures. Material considered rubbish by one group may be fuel, fertilizer, building fill, or a resource for craft production in another. Even the place chosen for disposal may carry meanings that are not obvious to an excavator. Ethnographic and historical sources can suggest possibilities, but analogy must not replace evidence from the site itself.

The archaeology of refuse is powerful precisely because it is necessarily incomplete. Gaps force researchers to combine context, recovery technique, measurement, comparison, and uncertainty. New laboratory methods can identify residues or microscopic wear, but they do not remove the need for contextual judgment. A trash pit cannot tell a household’s whole story, but it can preserve choices and routines rarely celebrated in monuments or written records.', 450, 13, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000024'::uuid, 'making-city-water-safe', 'Making City Water Safer', 'Historia del saneamiento urbano', 'history', 'C1', 3, 'long', 'Rapid urban growth in the nineteenth century placed pressure on water systems designed for smaller populations. Wells stood near leaking pits, waste entered rivers, and crowded districts often lacked reliable drainage. Epidemics were commonly explained through miasma, the belief that foul-smelling air caused disease. That theory was wrong about transmission, but concern about smell still motivated some street cleaning and sewer construction.

Evidence linking contaminated water to disease accumulated unevenly. During an outbreak, investigators could compare where ill residents obtained water, but urban networks were complicated. Competing companies sometimes served neighboring houses through different pipes. This accidental variation allowed patterns to emerge that would have been difficult to create deliberately, although surviving household and company records were often incomplete or inconsistent across districts. Investigators therefore compared several kinds of evidence.

Accepting waterborne transmission did not immediately produce safe supplies. Cities needed intake points away from sewage, pipes that prevented contamination, filtration, and treatment. Slow sand filters removed suspended material and supported biological layers that reduced microbes. Later, chemical disinfection provided another barrier. No single measure was sufficient when distribution pipes still leaked or waste remained unmanaged.

Infrastructure required political and financial decisions. Large sewers and treatment works demanded taxes, long construction periods, and coordination across municipal boundaries. Debates concerned who would pay, which neighborhoods would be connected first, and whether services should be publicly or privately managed. Wealthier districts often received improvements first, while renters and informal settlements had less influence. Public health benefits were broad, but costs and disruptions were not distributed equally.

Measurement made systems more accountable. Laboratories tested water, engineers tracked pressure, and health departments mapped disease. Standards converted observations into thresholds that suppliers were expected to meet. Publishing results could expose repeated failures and support demands for investment. Yet testing only occasional samples could miss short contamination events. Officials had to decide where, how often, and for which organisms to test.

Sanitation also changed rivers downstream. Moving waste away from city streets could simply transfer pollution to another community. Fish deaths, odors, and contaminated irrigation water made those transfers increasingly visible. Treatment therefore expanded from protecting drinking-water intakes to reducing the environmental burden of discharged sewage. This shift required seeing the river not merely as a pipe carrying waste away but as an ecosystem and a shared resource.

Modern systems inherit these layered solutions and inequalities. Filtration, disinfection, sewer separation, monitoring, and watershed protection work together. Operators must also retain training, spare parts, emergency plans, and public trust. Aging pipes, extreme rainfall, and unequal service can reopen risks that appear solved. The history of sanitation shows that technical knowledge matters, but durable safety depends on maintenance, public institutions, and attention to who remains outside the network.', 450, 14, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000025'::uuid, 'searching-for-blue', 'The Search for a Stable Blue', 'Historia de pigmentos azules', 'arts', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'For painters, a beautiful color is useful only if it can be prepared, applied, and preserved. Stable blue pigments were historically difficult to obtain. Ground lapis lazuli produced a brilliant blue, but the stone traveled through long trade routes and was expensive. Artists and patrons sometimes reserved it for the most important parts of an image.

Other blue materials had drawbacks. Some changed color when mixed with particular substances, while plant-based dyes could fade in light. Workshops developed careful recipes and applied protective layers, but results still varied. A pigment’s reputation depended not only on its initial appearance but on how it aged.

The development of synthetic blues expanded access and gave artists new choices. It did not simply replace older materials; painters selected pigments for hue, texture, cost, symbolism, and compatibility. The history of blue therefore reflects chemistry and trade as much as artistic preference.', 146, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026'::uuid, 'writing-down-dance', 'Writing Down Movement', 'Sistemas de notación coreográfica', 'arts', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'Dance disappears as it is performed, so choreographers have long searched for ways to record movement. Written notation can preserve sequences, directions, timing, and relationships among dancers. Some systems use symbols placed on a staff, while others combine floor diagrams with verbal descriptions.

No notation captures a performance completely. The angle of a hand can be marked, but qualities such as tension, hesitation, or weight may depend on interpretation. Detailed scores also require specialized training to read. For this reason, dancers often combine notation with oral coaching, photographs, or video.

Recording does more than support reconstruction. A score can reveal patterns that are difficult to notice while watching at full speed, and it allows researchers to compare versions created in different periods. Notation is therefore less a replacement for performance than a tool for preserving and analyzing selected features of it.', 141, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000027'::uuid, 'oral-epics-in-performance', 'An Epic Recreated', 'Tradiciones épicas orales', 'arts', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'An oral epic may be described as a long story passed from one performer to another, but this description can suggest that each performance is a copy of a fixed original. In many traditions, singers compose while performing. They draw on known episodes, repeated phrases, rhythmic patterns, and character types, combining them in response to the occasion.

Formulaic language helps make this possible. A phrase that fits a familiar rhythm can be inserted while the performer plans what comes next. Repetition also helps listeners follow a story over many hours. The formulas are not necessarily signs of poor memory; they are resources that support fluency and variation.

Audiences influence the event. A performer may expand a battle scene for listeners who value heroic detail or shorten a genealogical section when time is limited. Local names and current concerns can enter an old narrative. Listeners may also request a familiar episode or respond aloud at expected moments. Two performances can therefore be recognizably the same epic while differing greatly in length and emphasis.

Recording a performance preserves one valuable version but changes how it may be understood. A written transcription removes voice, gesture, timing, and audience response, while an audio or video recording still captures only one particular occasion. Scholars compare multiple performances and learn about the wider social, cultural, and historical setting so that variation is treated as part of the art rather than as corruption of a perfect text.', 240, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000028'::uuid, 'lighting-a-museum', 'Light That Reveals and Damages', 'Conservación e iluminación museística', 'arts', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'Museums need light so visitors can see, but light can permanently damage sensitive objects. Energy absorbed by dyes, paper, and textiles can trigger chemical changes that fade colors or weaken fibers. The damage accumulates: low light for a long period may have an effect similar to brighter light for a shorter one.

Conservators manage exposure rather than attempting to eliminate it. They choose lamps that emit little ultraviolet radiation, limit brightness, and rotate fragile works off display. Motion sensors may keep a gallery dim until someone enters. Exhibition designers also use contrast and directed light so that an object remains legible at a lower overall level.

There is no single safe brightness for every collection. Stone and metal usually tolerate more exposure than watercolor or historic fabric. Decisions balance access with preservation, recognizing that displaying an object now consumes part of the limited light exposure it can safely receive over its lifetime.', 152, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000029'::uuid, 'patterns-that-carry-memory', 'Patterns That Carry Memory', 'Textiles como sistemas culturales', 'arts', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A woven pattern can function as decoration, technical solution, social marker, and record of exchange at the same time. The structure of a loom determines which movements are convenient, but weavers also adapt tools to achieve desired forms. Repeated geometric units may help maintain tension and count threads while producing a recognizable visual rhythm. Small irregularities can record decisions made during the work.

Meanings attached to patterns are rarely fixed everywhere. A motif associated with one community may be borrowed, altered, or sold in another. Colors can indicate age, occasion, rank, or simply access to particular dyes. The same cloth may also acquire a new meaning when given as a gift. Interpreting a textile therefore requires information about who made it, who used it, where it circulated, and when.

Museum labels have sometimes assigned one definitive meaning to a design based on a single collector’s note. Such explanations are attractive because they are concise, but they can freeze a flexible tradition. The collector may have misunderstood a translation or recorded one person’s view as universal. Contemporary weavers may disagree with the label, use the motif differently, or intentionally create a new interpretation.

Technical study adds another layer. Examining fibers, dye molecules, and thread direction can reveal repairs, imported materials, or changes made during weaving. It may also distinguish a later replacement from the original border. These findings do not replace knowledge held by makers. The strongest interpretation brings material analysis into conversation with oral history, visual comparison, and present-day practice.', 250, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030'::uuid, 'rooms-designed-to-listen', 'Rooms Designed to Listen', 'Acústica arquitectónica', 'arts', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A concert hall does not merely contain sound; its shape and surfaces participate in what listeners hear. Sound travels directly from a performer and also reaches the audience after reflecting from walls, ceilings, seats, and people. Reflections arriving very soon can strengthen clarity and fullness, while much later ones may be perceived as echoes. Their direction also affects whether sound feels spatially enveloping.

The ideal balance depends on the performance. Speech benefits from clarity because listeners must distinguish rapid consonants. Some orchestral music benefits from longer reverberation that blends tones, while extremely long reverberation can blur detail. Musicians also need to hear one another clearly enough to coordinate. A room praised for one use may frustrate another.

Architects adjust geometry and materials to manage reflection and absorption. Irregular surfaces scatter sound in several directions; heavy curtains and upholstered seats absorb more energy. Empty and occupied rooms can sound different because audiences themselves absorb sound. Temperature and humidity can produce smaller changes in how sound travels. Designers sometimes choose seats whose absorption resembles that of a person so rehearsals and performances remain more consistent.

Modern halls may include movable panels, curtains, or overhead reflectors. These elements allow one room to support lectures, chamber groups, and larger ensembles. Technicians can change configurations between events and document settings that worked well. Adjustable design does not create a perfect setting for every event, but it recognizes acoustics as a set of trade-offs rather than a single measure of quality.', 245, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'::uuid, 'restoring-what-remains', 'Restoring What Remains', 'Ética de restauración artística', 'arts', 'B2', 3, 'long', 'When an artwork is damaged, restoration does not simply return it to an obvious original state. Materials age, earlier repairs alter surfaces, and evidence about the artist’s intention may be incomplete. Different owners may also value different periods in the object’s life. Conservators must decide what kind of change is justified, visible, reversible, and documented.

Cleaning illustrates the difficulty. Removing darkened varnish can recover contrast and color, but the boundary between varnish and original glaze may be uncertain. A solvent strong enough to remove one layer can affect another. Earlier cleanings may already have thinned delicate paint. Conservators test small areas and examine samples, yet every cleaning still involves judgment about which traces of age belong to the object’s history.

Missing areas create a different problem. Leaving a loss untouched makes damage visible but can interrupt the image. Filling it completely may produce a visually continuous work while giving modern additions too much authority. Large losses can require structural support even when no image is reconstructed. Some approaches use retouching that blends at normal viewing distance but becomes distinguishable under close inspection or special light.

Reversibility is often treated as an ethical goal. A repair material should ideally be removable without harming the original, allowing future conservators to respond to new evidence or better technology. Perfect reversibility is rare, however. Even a removable coating may change how an old surface ages, and the act of removing it can create stress. Conservators therefore also consider compatibility and predictable aging.

The history of an object may include previous restorations. An old addition can be technically inaccurate yet culturally important because viewers have known the work in that form for generations. Removing it may reveal earlier material while erasing part of the object’s later biography. Ownership, law, and ritual use can shape who participates in the decision. Conservators consult curators, historians, scientists, owners, and sometimes communities connected to the work.

Digital reconstruction offers another option. A projection or screen can show a proposed original appearance without placing paint on the object. Digital models can also compare several hypotheses. Displayed simulations may be updated when evidence changes. They are not neutral: choices about color, missing detail, and certainty still shape what viewers see. Clear labels should distinguish evidence from speculation.

Good restoration is therefore not measured by making intervention invisible at any cost. It aims to stabilize material, support understanding, and remain honest about uncertainty. Detailed records preserve photographs, tests, materials, and reasoning so future researchers can evaluate not only the artwork but the decisions made around it. Records also identify areas that should be monitored after treatment and note changing environmental conditions that could affect the repair over time in storage or transit.', 450, 13, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000032'::uuid, 'photographs-and-public-memory', 'Photographs and Public Memory', 'Fotografía como evidencia y memoria', 'arts', 'C1', 3, 'long', 'Photographs often appear to preserve a moment automatically: light from a scene reached a camera and left a trace. This physical connection gives photographs evidentiary force, but it does not make them complete or self-explanatory. A scene can also be carefully staged before exposure. A frame includes some people and excludes others, fixes one instant, and reflects where a photographer was able or permitted to stand.

Captions guide interpretation. A date, location, and name can connect an image to other records, while an inaccurate caption can redirect meaning for decades. Names may be absent because an institution never asked or because a record was separated from the print. Captions also classify. Describing a group as residents, protesters, workers, or a crowd foregrounds different relationships. Archives increasingly preserve the history of caption changes rather than treating the latest wording as timeless.

Survival is selective. Institutions preserved some photographs because they documented officials, major construction, or celebrated events. Family albums and commercial studios preserved other views, but humidity, displacement, and private decisions destroyed many collections. Digitization can widen access to surviving material without restoring what was never kept. The visual record may therefore be abundant and still represent some communities or activities poorly.

Reproduction changes significance. A small photograph in an album can become a large museum print, a newspaper illustration, or a rapidly shared digital image. Cropping may remove surrounding details; contrast adjustments may reveal or conceal features. Search systems can also favor images with detailed captions over poorly described ones. Repeated publication can turn one image into a symbol for an event even when it was not typical of what most participants experienced.

People photographed are not merely subjects for later interpretation. Images may expose grief, medical conditions, sacred practices, or identities that create risk. Legal permission to publish does not settle every ethical question, especially when the photographer’s consent was obtained under unequal conditions or when descendants understand the image differently. Access decisions may need periodic review as circumstances change.

Communities and archives have developed responses. Some projects invite people to identify relatives, correct descriptions, record oral histories, or set culturally appropriate access restrictions. These collaborations can add knowledge unavailable in institutional files. Respectful participation also requires time, compensation, and clarity about who controls the result. It can reveal disagreement within a community, so participation should not be represented as producing a single collective voice.

Reading a historical photograph therefore involves movement between image and context. Researchers inspect visible details but also ask who made the image, how it circulated, why it survived, what information accompanied it, and who can speak about it now. Comparing related frames or records can test an interpretation. The photograph remains evidence, but its evidentiary value grows when uncertainty and the history of its use are made visible.', 464, 14, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000033'::uuid, 'clocks-and-escapements', 'Releasing Time One Step at a Time', 'Mecanismos de relojería', 'technology', 'B1', 1, 'short', 'A mechanical clock needs stored energy, often supplied by a falling weight or wound spring. If that energy were released freely, the gears would spin quickly and stop. The escapement solves this problem by alternately locking and releasing a gear in small, regular steps.

Each release gives a pendulum or balance wheel a slight push. The oscillator then controls when the next tooth can pass. The familiar ticking sound comes from these repeated contacts. Accuracy depends on the steadiness of the oscillator as well as the shape, friction, and alignment of the parts.

No escapement is perfectly isolated from its environment. Temperature can change the dimensions of components, oil can thicken, and motion can disturb a portable watch. Later designs and materials reduced these effects. Their goal was not to create energy but to divide its release into intervals that remained as uniform as possible.', 145, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000034'::uuid, 'open-source-repair', 'Instructions That Remain Open', 'Diseño abierto y reparabilidad', 'technology', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'An open hardware project publishes enough design information for others to study, modify, and build the device. Files may include diagrams, circuit layouts, software, parts lists, and assembly instructions. Access to a drawing alone is not always sufficient; a useful project also states versions, tolerances, and testing procedures.

Openness can support repair. When a component fails, a technician can identify it and understand how it interacts with the rest of the system. Communities may propose replacement parts after the original component is no longer sold. Shared documentation also lets users discover recurring failures and improve later versions.

Open design does not guarantee that repair will be easy or safe. Specialized tools, high voltage, sealed materials, or unavailable components can still create barriers. Licenses explain what others may legally do with the files, while practical documentation explains what they can realistically accomplish. Both are important, but they solve different problems.', 149, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000035'::uuid, 'keeping-food-cold', 'A Chain That Must Stay Cold', 'Logística de refrigeración', 'technology', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A cold chain keeps temperature-sensitive products within a specified range from production to use. It may include precooling at a farm, refrigerated storage, insulated vehicles, transfer centers, retail cabinets, and household refrigerators. The chain is only as reliable as its weakest transfer, because a product can warm rapidly while waiting on an uncovered loading platform.

Temperature history matters more than a reading taken at the destination. Some damage accumulates gradually, while crossing a critical threshold can allow rapid microbial growth or alter a medicine. Small data loggers travel with shipments and record conditions over time. Their position inside a box should reflect the product rather than the coldest nearby surface. Fixed sensors monitor warehouses and vehicles, but they may not represent the temperature inside every package.

Packaging can slow heat transfer but cannot provide unlimited cooling. Ice packs, phase-change materials, insulation, and airflow must be selected for the route and product. Packing boxes too tightly can block cold air in a refrigerated truck; leaving excessive empty space can permit shifting and damage.

Operators prepare for failures such as delayed flights, power cuts, and broken doors. A response plan identifies alternative storage, safe time limits, and who can decide whether goods remain usable. Staff rehearse transfers so emergency equipment can be found and activated quickly. Discarding everything after a minor deviation creates waste, but accepting products without evidence can create health risks. Good cold-chain management combines prevention with documented decisions after an interruption.', 242, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000036'::uuid, 'why-keyboards-keep-their-shape', 'Why Keyboards Keep Their Shape', 'Persistencia de diseños tecnológicos', 'technology', 'B1', 2, 'short', 'Early typewriters used mechanical arms that struck an inked ribbon. If nearby arms were activated in rapid succession, they could collide or jam. Keyboard layouts distributed commonly paired letters across the mechanism, though the history of any particular arrangement involved several design revisions and commercial choices.

Once typists learned a layout, changing it imposed a cost. Schools taught it, manufacturers produced compatible machines, and workplaces evaluated speed using familiar patterns. Later technologies no longer had the same mechanical constraints, but the network of trained users and established equipment remained.

Alternative layouts may offer advantages for particular languages, tasks, or accessibility needs. Their existence does not automatically cause a universal switch. A technology can persist because coordination is valuable: an imperfect shared standard may be more useful than several incompatible improvements.', 130, 5, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000037'::uuid, 'seeing-storms-from-orbit', 'Seeing Storms from Orbit', 'Observación meteorológica satelital', 'technology', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'Weather satellites observe large areas repeatedly, including oceans and remote regions with few ground stations. Some remain above nearly the same longitude, providing frequent images of one broad region. Their rapid updates are useful for following the development of storms. Others orbit closer to Earth and pass over different locations, producing more detailed observations but revisiting a given place less continuously.

Satellite instruments measure radiation in visible, infrared, and microwave bands. Visible images show reflected sunlight and therefore depend on daylight. Infrared measurements estimate temperatures of cloud tops or surfaces and work at night. Certain microwave signals can pass through clouds and provide information about rain, moisture, sea ice, or ocean winds. Each band responds to a different combination of atmospheric and surface properties.

An image is not a direct photograph in every band. Computers convert measured radiation into colors, and the chosen color scale emphasizes particular features. The same measurement can be displayed with several palettes for different tasks. Forecasters compare several channels and ground observations rather than interpreting one bright area in isolation. Models also use satellite data to estimate the current atmospheric state before calculating a forecast.

Satellites have limitations. They infer many quantities indirectly, instruments age, and clouds or surface properties can complicate retrieval. Calibration against trusted surface measurements remains necessary throughout the mission. Instrument teams compare overlapping observations as satellites cross. The value of satellite observation comes from broad, repeated coverage combined with—not substituted for—other independent scientific evidence.', 243, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000038'::uuid, 'sensors-in-a-river', 'Sensors in a River', 'Monitoreo ambiental con sensores', 'technology', 'B2', 2, 'standard', 'A water-quality sensor can record temperature, conductivity, oxygen, or turbidity at intervals much shorter than occasional manual sampling. Frequent records reveal daily cycles and brief events, such as a muddy pulse after rain, that a monthly visit could miss. A sequence can also show whether conditions recover after the event. Sensors are especially useful where change happens quickly.

Continuous data are not automatically reliable. Algae and sediment grow or settle on sensor surfaces, gradually shifting readings. Electronic components drift, batteries weaken, and communication can fail. Technicians clean instruments, compare them with reference standards, and inspect patterns for sudden jumps or implausibly constant values. Field notes help separate maintenance effects from real environmental change.

Placement matters. A sensor near a pipe may detect a discharge but not represent the full river. One near the bank may experience different flow from the center. Seasonal changes in water level can also leave an instrument in an unsuitable position. Networks use several locations and combine fixed instruments with laboratory samples. Metadata record depth, calibration, maintenance, and periods when a reading should be treated cautiously.

Public dashboards can make river conditions visible in near real time, but a number without context may cause confusion. A temporary rise in turbidity after a storm is not equivalent to a toxic spill. Historical ranges help users judge whether a value is unusual for that season. Effective systems pair measurements with thresholds, explanations, uncertainty, and contact information for unusual events.', 241, 7, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000039'::uuid, 'printing-as-a-system', 'Printing as a System', 'Producción técnica de libros impresos', 'technology', 'B2', 3, 'long', 'Movable-type printing is often represented by the press itself, but repeatable book production depended on a larger technical system. Printers needed durable type, consistent ink, suitable paper, skilled compositors, reliable presses, and markets large enough to recover the cost of preparing a text. A shortage in any one element could delay the entire production plan.

Type had to combine uniformity with flexibility. Individual letters were cast so they could be arranged into lines, locked into a frame, inked, printed, cleaned, and reused. If pieces differed too much in height, some letters printed darkly while others barely touched the paper. Foundries organized thousands of pieces by character and size. Metal alloys balanced hardness, casting detail, and the need to remelt worn type.

Compositors assembled text by hand, selecting letters from divided cases. They had to read while working backward relative to the printed impression and manage spacing so lines fit a chosen width. Decisions about abbreviation and line breaks affected both labor and appearance. A completed page form contained not only letters but blank pieces that created spaces and held the structure tightly together.

Ink developed for writing did not automatically work on metal type. Thin ink could run off the raised surfaces or spread into paper; printing ink needed enough body to transfer a clear image. Paper also varied in thickness, moisture, and strength. Dampening could improve printing but had to be controlled sheet by sheet. Printers adjusted pressure and packing rather than assuming every sheet would behave identically.

Errors could enter at many stages. A compositor might select the wrong letter, type could shift, a page could be imposed in the wrong position, or sheets could be folded incorrectly. Proofreading corrected some mistakes before a full run, but stopping production and resetting type cost time. Lists of corrections could be added when resetting was impractical. Surviving copies of the same edition sometimes contain different corrections made during printing.

The economics shaped design. Large initial labor made longer print runs cheaper per copy, but unsold books tied up capital. Size, paper quality, illustrations, and the number of copies all changed financial risk. Printers cooperated with publishers, booksellers, authors, institutions, and sometimes governments that granted privileges or imposed censorship. Technical capacity did not by itself determine what reached readers.

Printing increased the number of comparatively consistent copies, yet it did not produce perfect uniformity. Hand work, material variation, correction, binding, and later use gave copies individual histories. Libraries sometimes combined sheets from different states when repairing volumes. Owners also annotated, trimmed, rebound, or rearranged their surviving personal copies after purchase. Understanding printing as a system explains both its power to reproduce text and the differences that remain within apparently identical books.', 450, 13, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true),
    ('60000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000040'::uuid, 'microgrids-that-can-island', 'A Grid That Can Become an Island', 'Microredes eléctricas resilientes', 'technology', 'C1', 3, 'long', 'A microgrid is a local electrical network containing loads and one or more energy resources. Under normal conditions it may exchange electricity with the larger utility grid, buying power or exporting a surplus. If the external network fails, some microgrids can disconnect at a controlled point and continue operating independently, a mode known as islanding. Others are designed only for connected operation.

Islanding requires more than adding solar panels. A grid-connected system usually relies on the wider network to stabilize voltage and frequency. Once separated, local controls must perform that task. At least one resource must respond quickly when demand or generation changes, and protective devices must distinguish faults inside the microgrid from problems beyond it. These controls need testing under realistic transitions.

Energy storage is useful because it can absorb or deliver power rapidly. A battery may smooth short fluctuations and support the transition into island mode. Its energy capacity is limited, however. Usable capacity also declines with age and extreme temperature. Several hours of cloudy weather or high demand can exhaust it unless other generators operate or some loads are reduced. Operators track both power and remaining energy.

Microgrids therefore classify loads by priority. A hospital might maintain emergency rooms, refrigeration, and communications while temporarily disconnecting offices or nonessential equipment. Automated controls can shed loads quickly, but priorities require human decisions made before an emergency. Different users may disagree about what is essential, especially when an outage lasts for days. Published plans can make those choices more accountable.

Renewable generation introduces forecasts as well as uncertainty. Solar and wind reduce fuel use, but their output varies. Controllers combine weather predictions, current battery charge, expected demand, and fuel availability to decide whether to store energy or use it. They may preserve extra charge before a predicted storm. A forecast error does not necessarily cause failure if the system retains adequate reserve.

Connection and reconnection must be managed carefully. An islanded microgrid cannot simply close a switch if its voltage phase and frequency differ from the utility network. Controls synchronize the two systems first. Utility workers also need clear indication that local wires may remain energized during a wider outage. Protection settings must coordinate so a local fault is isolated without unnecessarily shutting down every critical load.

The value of a microgrid depends on more than whether it survives one demonstration. Equipment needs maintenance, software must be secured, fuel contracts may be needed, and operators require training. Replacement parts and technical support must remain reliably available years after installation. Financing must cover operation as well as construction. A technically advanced system that a community cannot afford to maintain may provide less resilience than a simpler design supported by durable local institutions.', 451, 14, 'published', 1, 'Contenido original Macitta; revisión lingüística, factual y de fairness v1.', true, true, true)
